{
  "dom_id": "step-q183967",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+5,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,n\\in N,\n$$\n\nuse mathematical induction to prove the following statement: \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5n-2,\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side of our statement, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}={{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**:Using the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}+5 & ={{field-10}} \\\\  & ={{field-11}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}=5n-2$ is true for all {{field-12}}.",
